# rules: go-context-cancel-not-called
# languages: go
# fixture: go/CWE-459.go
# from: CWE-459 (Incomplete Cleanup), CWE-772, go vet (lostcancel)
#
# WithCancel, WithTimeout and WithDeadline each return a cancel function, and
# the context they return stays attached to its parent until that function is
# called. Dropping it leaks the child, and everything watching it, for as long
# as the parent lives - which for a server's background context is the life of
# the process. A timeout does not save it: WithTimeout releases its own timer
# when it fires, but the parent keeps the child until cancel runs.
#
# Discarding the function into `_` and never calling a function that was named
# are the same bug, and one reading finds both: `_` can never be the name in a
# later `defer _()`, so the subtraction that removes the released cases leaves
# it behind on its own.
#
# The release has to be a sibling of the derivation. A cancel called inside an
# if, or handed to something else to call, is reported.

["$CTX, $CANCEL := context.WithCancel($$$_)",
 "$CTX, $CANCEL := context.WithTimeout($$$_)",
 "$CTX, $CANCEL := context.WithDeadline($$$_)"] as $derive
| ( ($derive | map(. + "\n$$$_\ndefer $C()"))
    + ($derive | map(. + "\n$$$_\n$C()")) ) as $released
| scan_ast("*.go"; $derive + $released) as $all
| ( ($all | of($derive) | focus("CANCEL"))
    | not_at($all | of($released) | where_same("CANCEL"; "C") | focus("CANCEL")) )
| finding("go-context-cancel-not-called";
    "the cancel function returned with $CTX is never called, so $CTX stays "
    + "attached to its parent until the parent is cancelled")
| report
